    "math_academy_id": "q-75611",
    "knowledge_point_id": "b7f5608f-a9f5-5e6a-b308-540ed8b29259",
    "knowledge_point": "Determining the Value of an Unknown Given an Eigenvalue and Eigenvector of a Matrix",
    "problem": "The eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 20-4k & 3\\end{aligned}]$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2k+1$ is  $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Determine the value of $k.$",
    "worked_solution": "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\lambda v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 20-4k & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] & =(2k+1)[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 26-4k\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2k+1 \\\\ 4k+2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}7=2k+1, \\\\ 26-4k=4k+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $k$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n7=2k+1\\,⟹\\,k=3.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $k=3$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}26-4k & =4k+2 \\\\ 26-4\\cdot 3 & =4\\cdot 3+2 \\\\ 14 & =14,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a true statement.\n\nTherefore, the solution of the system is $k=3.$",
